# Contributing to OpenDCKit

This file is the companion to the [OpenDCKit Technical Charter](). Where the charter says a
thing is documented "in the CONTRIBUTING file," this is that file.

The community owns this document. Changes to it are proposed as pull requests and
approved by the TSC.

> **Draft.** The maintainer list in [MAINTAINERS.md](MAINTAINERS.md) is empty and needs to be
> filled in and agreed by the founding group before the charter is adopted, because the charter
> seats the initial TSC from it. Links marked TBD are created during project onboarding.

## Code of conduct

Everyone participating in OpenDCKit is expected to follow the
[LF Projects Code of Conduct](https://lfprojects.org/policies/code-of-conduct/).
Report concerns to the TSC chair or to conduct@lfprojects.org.

## Roles

**Contributor.** Anyone who contributes code, documentation, tests, reviews, or other
technical artifacts. No approval or paperwork is required to start. Open an issue or a pull
request.

**Maintainer.** A contributor who has earned commit access to the repository. Maintainers
review and merge pull requests, triage issues, and are responsible for the health of the
areas they look after.

**TSC.** The Technical Steering Committee holds technical oversight of the project. Its
voting members are the active maintainers, one vote each.

## Maintainers

The maintainer list lives in [MAINTAINERS.md](MAINTAINERS.md) and is incorporated into this
file by reference. Active maintainers in that file are the voting members of the TSC.

Maintainers are listed as individuals, not as company seats. A maintainer who changes
employer keeps their role.

### Becoming a maintainer

Any contributor with a sustained track record of quality contributions and reviews may be
nominated. Nomination is an issue opened by an existing maintainer, and the change takes a
majority vote of the TSC, recorded by a pull request against MAINTAINERS.md. There is no
fixed contribution count; judgment sits with the TSC.

### Stepping down and removal

Maintainers may step down at any time by opening a pull request against MAINTAINERS.md. A
maintainer who has been inactive for an extended period may be moved to emeritus by a
majority vote of the TSC, and can be reinstated the same way.

## How to contribute

1. Open an issue first for anything larger than a bug fix, so the design gets discussed
   before the code is written.
2. Fork the repository and work on a branch.
3. Keep pull requests focused. One logical change per pull request reviews faster than a
   large mixed one.
4. Include tests for behavior changes, and update documentation in the same pull request.
5. Sign off every commit (see below).
6. Open the pull request and respond to review comments.

### Sign-off (DCO)

Every commit must carry a Developer Certificate of Origin sign-off. Use `git commit -s`,
which appends a line like:

```
Signed-off-by: Jane Doe <jane@example.com>
```

By signing off you are certifying the statement at <https://developercertificate.org>. The
name and email must match the commit author. There is no CLA to sign.

If you forget, `git commit --amend -s` fixes the last commit, and
`git rebase --signoff` fixes a branch.

### Review and merge

Pull requests need approval from at least one maintainer other than the author before
merging. Maintainers do not merge their own pull requests without another maintainer's
approval. If a reviewer requests changes, that objection is resolved before the pull
request merges rather than outvoted.

Disagreements that reviewers cannot resolve between themselves go to the TSC.

## Licensing

Code contributions are made under the [Apache License 2.0](LICENSE). Documentation is made
available under [CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Contributors
retain copyright in their contributions; nothing is assigned to the project.

Add an SPDX identifier to new source files:

```
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
```

Any dependency added must be compatible with Apache-2.0. Use of a different license for an
inbound or outbound contribution requires an exception approved by a two-thirds vote of the
TSC, as set out in the charter.

## Reporting a security issue

Do not open a public issue for a suspected vulnerability. Report it to TBD. The community
will acknowledge the report and coordinate disclosure with the reporter.

## Meetings and communication

- Chat: <https://linuxfoundation.zulipchat.com>
- Meetings: all public meetings are on the
  [Community Meeting Calendar](https://zoom-lfx.platform.linuxfoundation.org/meetings/opendckit?view=week).
  To request a new meeting, email <support@lfnetworking.org> with the meeting name, its
  committee, working group, or SIG (if any), the cadence, and the time, including the time
  zone (UTC, PT, etc.)
- Mailing list: TBD
- Issues and pull requests: <https://github.com/opendckit>
- Infrastructure and tooling requests: <support@lfnetworking.org>

Technical discussion happens in the open. Decisions made in a meeting or a chat are
summarized back into the relevant issue or pull request so that people who were not present
can follow along.

## TSC decision making

The project works by consensus. Where a vote is needed, it is one vote per TSC voting
member, and quorum is half of the voting members. Most decisions carry on a majority; charter
amendments and license exceptions need two thirds of the entire TSC. Details are in the
charter, which governs if this summary and the charter ever disagree.
